--TEST--
a forwarded header from an untrusted peer is not believed, and is still readable `rule:http-server/trusted-proxies-is-empty-and-empty-reads-nothing` `rule:security/tainted-sources`
--CLIENT_IP--
203.0.113.7
--HEADERS--
X-Forwarded-For: 198.51.100.9
X-Forwarded-Proto: https
Host: example.test
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: tools:server/behind-a-proxy-trusted-proxies
// `[server] trusted_proxies` defaults to empty, and empty means these two lines
// were never read: the request below states what the walk settled on, which is
// the peer's own address over the connection's own scheme. A member that
// re-read the headers would answer `198.51.100.9` and `https` here.
echo Core\Request::clientIp() ?? "none", "\n";
echo Core\Request::scheme(), "\n";
// They are still readable, `tainted`, for an application that wants to decide
// for itself: the rule withholds belief, not the bytes.
echo Core\Request::header("x-forwarded-for") ?? "none", "\n";
echo Core\Request::header("x-forwarded-proto") ?? "none", "\n";
// The authority is the `Host` line and nothing forwarded, there being no
// `X-Forwarded-Host` read anywhere — deriving an origin from one is
// host-header injection.
echo Core\Request::host() ?? "none", "\n";
--EXPECT--
203.0.113.7
http
198.51.100.9
https
example.test
